umbent combines a dominant money advantage, law-enforcement and editorial endorsements, and a Republican nominee who won the primary despite having dropped out and has barely fundraised since. Raters and markets align on a clear Democratic favorite in a district Trump carried, supporting a double-digit expected margin.", "watch": "A late surge of Republican outside spending or an active campaign by Smith could narrow what is otherwise a low-information race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Kristen McDonald Rivet (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Kristen McDonald Rivet, the Democratic incumbent, seeks a second term in a mid-Michigan district that Donald Trump carried and that leans about a point Republican. She has raised $6.8 million and holds $3.2 million on hand, while Republican nominee Thomas Smith has raised roughly $550,000. Smith won the August primary despite having suspended his campaign, defeating a Trump-backed rival, and has shown limited fundraising since. A Green, a Libertarian, and an independent candidate also appear on the ballot.\n\nNo public polls cover the race, but ten expert ratings range from Likely D to Solid or Safe D, and prediction markets give the incumbent about a 94 percent chance of winning. The Fraternal Order of Police has endorsed her, and editorial boards have backed her reelection. The combination of incumbency, financial dominance, and a weakened Republican nominee makes the district a Safe Democratic bet despite its Republican lean.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9646, "margin": 12.11}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9589, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9779, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9667, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9568, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9588, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9559, "margi